  "judge": {
    "dimensions": [
      {
        "name": "Correctness",
        "score": 9,
        "rationale": "The agent removed the top-level `concurrency` group with `cancel-in-progress: true`, so a second run for the same sha can no longer cancel the first. The `test` job id is unchanged. The explanation is correct but brief: `opened` and `labeled` both fire on the same head commit, one run gets cancelled, and that cancelled run can end up as the `test` result on the commit. The required check then counts as not passing and blocks the merge even though no step failed. The new test passed, and the agent confirmed it fails against the original file. The agent also said honestly that the fix still needs to be confirmed on GitHub."
      },
      {
        "name": "Restraint",
        "score": 5,
        "rationale": "There are several edits beyond the minimal fix. First, the agent dropped `labeled` from the `pull_request` types. That changes behaviour: any label-driven re-run of CI stops working. Removing the concurrency group alone already fixes the bug, though the agent did flag this change openly. Second, it added a new test file and a comment, which are related but go beyond what was asked. Third, a compiled `tests/__pycache__/*.pyc` binary was staged into the diff. That file has nothing to do with the fix and should not be there."
      }
    ],
    "overall": 7.0
  },
